# 5. FIX THE DIAGONAL LINE PATTERN

The existing diagonal lines are one of the primary causes of the visible seams.

They currently appear to restart at section boundaries.

Move the pattern into a single global texture layer.

### Requirements

The lines must:

- Use one common origin.
- Maintain consistent angle.
- Maintain consistent spacing.
- Maintain consistent thickness.
- Continue across every section boundary.
- Avoid independent section-level repetition.
- Avoid changes in opacity at arbitrary section edges.

### Suggested implementation

```css
.page-atmosphere__lines {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;

  background-image:
    repeating-linear-gradient(
      112deg,
      transparent 0,
      transparent 150px,
      rgba(130, 145, 180, 0.045) 150px,
      rgba(130, 145, 180, 0.045) 151px,
      transparent 151px,
      transparent 230px
    );

  background-position: 0 0;
  pointer-events: none;
}
```

Treat these values as starting points.

The final result should use very subtle lines.

The lines must be decorative rather than visually dominant.

### Critical rule

Do not reapply the same repeating gradient to every section.

Even if two sections use identical gradient CSS, their individual background origins can cause visible discontinuities.

A single page-level layer eliminates those local resets.

### Optional variation

Use a page-level opacity mask to gently reduce line visibility in text-heavy areas.

However, the mask must transition smoothly over broad distances.

Do not create hard horizontal fade boundaries that correspond to individual sections.

The line field should feel continuous even where its intensity decreases.

