# 14. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS

The new shared background must not negatively affect performance.

### CSS

Avoid applying backdrop-filter to entire full-width sections.

Use it primarily on individual glass components.

Avoid unnecessary stacking contexts and repeated large blur filters.

### WebGL

If WebGL is used:

- Prefer one background renderer.
- Avoid unnecessary extra canvases.
- Use shared geometry and materials.
- Keep device pixel ratio reasonable.
- Reduce shader quality on low-powered devices.
- Avoid per-frame resource allocations.
- Handle context loss safely.
- Dispose of unused GPU resources.

### Animation

Reduce unnecessary background updates when the page is not visible.

Use a single animation clock where possible.

Do not continuously recalculate full document layout in every animation frame.

### Fallback

If WebGL is unavailable, the CSS background should still create one continuous, attractive glassmorphic environment.

Do not fall back to separately applied gradients on each section.

